# ── Server-only secrets for the phone-verification API (api/otp/*) ────────────
# Copy this file to `.env.local` (already git-ignored) and fill in the values,
# or copy the same names into Vercel → Settings → Environment Variables.
#
# NEVER prefix any of these with VITE_ — Vite inlines every VITE_* variable into
# the browser bundle, which would hand the key to every visitor of the site.
#
# This template is tracked. Keep every value blank here; real values belong in
# `.env.local` (git-ignored) or Vercel → Settings → Environment Variables.

# ── SMS rails ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two providers, chosen by the destination's dial code in `api/_lib/sms.js`:
#
#   Yoola        → Uganda (+256), Kenya (+254), Tanzania (+255), Rwanda (+250),
#                  Malawi (+265), Zambia (+260)
#   eSMS Africa  → every other country, including all 54 African countries and
#                  the rest of the world (some destinations are enabled on request)
#
# Both keys are server-only. A blank key makes the send endpoint answer 503 for
# the numbers that need that rail; it never fakes a send.

# ── Yoola SMS (yoolasms.com → Developers / API) ───────────────────────────────
# Paste the live key here or in `.env.local`; never commit it.
YOOLA_API_KEY=

# The sender ID (the name the SMS arrives from), once Yoola has approved it.
# Leave blank to send from whatever your account defaults to.
YOOLA_SENDER_ID=

# Where the send endpoint lives. Only change this if Yoola moves you.
YOOLA_API_URL=https://yoolasms.com/api/v1/send_sms

# ── eSMS Africa (sms.esmsafrica.io → API Keys) ───────────────────────────────
# Live keys start esms_live_; test keys start esms_test_ and simulate delivery.
# Create or rotate one at https://sms.esmsafrica.io/api-keys.
ESMS_API_KEY=

# Optional approved sender ID. Leave blank to use the route default. A sender
# that is not approved for the destination country makes eSMS answer 403.
ESMS_SENDER_ID=

# eSMS's send endpoint. Routes are auto-detected from the E.164 number, so one
# URL covers every country.
ESMS_API_URL=https://sms.esmsafrica.io/api/messages/send

# ── One-time codes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A long random string the codes are hashed with, so the database never holds a
# code anyone could read. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# Changing it invalidates every code in flight at that moment — which is the
# whole point of owning it.
OTP_PEPPER=your_otp_pepper_here

# The same pepper keys contact discovery. A selected contact number is HMAC'd to
# the same opaque key as `phones/{phoneKey}` before lookup, so neither the request
# nor the database ever holds a readable address book. Rotating it invalidates the
# existing phone ledger too — rotate only with a planned re-verification.

# The most codes the server will send in one day (UTC). A cost rail, not a
# target: 400 × UGX 35 ≈ UGX 14,000 on Yoola's six countries; eSMS prices vary
# by destination. Raise it deliberately, never by accident.
OTP_DAILY_CAP=400

# The host customers open the site at, with no scheme and no path. Chrome's WebOTP
# auto-fill only offers a text message to the exact host in its final `@host #code`
# line, so `www.` matters. Leave this as rachett.com for production; use
# localhost only if you are testing the listener on a local HTTPS build.
OTP_WEB_HOST=rachett.com

# ── Firebase Admin (the server's own identity) ────────────────────────────────
# Local development: a path to the downloaded service-account file.
GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=.secrets/service-account.json

# On Vercel there are no files, so the key travels as a variable instead.
# One line, no newline damage:  base64 -w0 .secrets/service-account.json
FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_BASE64=

# Same thing, if you would rather paste the JSON itself (keep the \n escapes).
FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON=
